confidence in professional situations, you're in the right place.

5
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:34,580
That's right, and today we have a very powerful and practical topic that can completely change

6
00:00:34,580 --> 00:00:40,900
your career. Absolutely. Today we're talking about how to improve presentation skills in business

7
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:47,780
English. This is such an important skill. Whether you're in meetings, giving reports, pitching ideas,

8
00:00:47,780 --> 00:00:54,500
or even introducing yourself, presentation skills matter a lot. Exactly. And the good news is,

9
00:00:54,500 --> 00:01:01,540
this is a skill you can learn and improve with practice. Yes, nobody is born a perfect presenter.

10
00:01:01,540 --> 00:01:04,040
Everyone improves step by step.

11
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:07,380
And today, we're going to help you do exactly that

12
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:11,180
with real examples, simple phrases, and practical situations.

13
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:12,340
Sounds great!

14
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:14,740
So before we start, let me ask you,

15
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:17,080
do you enjoy giving presentations?

16
00:01:17,080 --> 00:01:19,880
Honestly, I used to be very nervous.

17
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:22,840
My hands would shake and I would forget what to say.

18
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:26,040
That's very common. Many people feel the same way.

19
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:28,640
But over time, I learned some techniques

20
00:01:28,640 --> 00:01:30,280
and now I actually enjoy it.

21
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:31,600
That's encouraging.

22
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:33,720
Alright, everyone, let's begin.

23
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:35,400
Okay, first question.

24
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:38,800
What makes a good presentation in business English?

25
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:44,280
A good presentation is clear, structured, engaging, and confident.

26
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:45,840
Let's break that down.

27
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:47,560
What do you mean by clear?

28
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:50,560
Clear means your message is easy to understand.

29
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:54,520
You don't use complicated words or long confusing sentences.

30
00:01:54,520 --> 00:01:56,920
So simple English is better?

31
00:01:56,920 --> 00:01:58,080
Exactly.

32
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:01,080
Simple and direct is always better in business communication.

33
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:03,080
And what about structure?

34
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:08,080
Structure means your presentation has a beginning, middle, and end.

35
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:09,080
Like a story?

36
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:11,080
Yes, exactly like a story.

37
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:15,080
You introduce your topic, explain your ideas, and then summarize.

38
00:02:15,080 --> 00:02:18,080
That makes it easier for the audience to follow.

39
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:22,080
Right. Now let's talk about something very important. The opening.

40
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:25,080
Yes, first impressions matter.

41
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:29,080
When you start your presentation, you need to sound confident and professional.

42
00:02:29,080 --> 00:02:32,080
Can you give some examples of good opening lines?

43
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:33,080
Sure. You can say,

44
00:02:33,080 --> 00:02:36,080
Good morning, everyone. Thank you for being here today.

45
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:38,080
That sounds very natural.

46
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:42,080
Or, today I'm going to talk about our sales performance for this quarter.

47
00:02:42,080 --> 00:02:44,080
Simple and clear.

48
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:49,080
Exactly. Avoid starting with um or so.

49
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:51,080
Yes, that doesn't sound professional.

50
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:54,080
Now, let's imagine a real situation.

51
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:56,080
You're presenting in a meeting.

52
00:02:56,080 --> 00:02:57,580
Okay, let me try.

53
00:02:57,580 --> 00:02:58,580
Go ahead.

54
00:02:58,580 --> 00:03:00,080
Good afternoon, everyone.

55
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:03,080
Today I'd like to present our new marketing strategy.

56
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:05,580
Excellent. That's confident and clear.

57
00:03:05,580 --> 00:03:06,580
Thank you.

58
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:08,580
Now let's move to the next part.

59
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:10,580
Organizing your ideas.

60
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:12,580
This is where many people struggle.

61
00:03:12,580 --> 00:03:13,580
Yes.

62
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:17,580
One helpful technique is to use simple sign posting language.

63
00:03:17,580 --> 00:03:19,080
What does that mean?

64
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:22,580
It means using phrases to guide your audience through your presentation.

65
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:24,580
Can you give examples?

66
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:27,580
Sure. You can say, first, I'll talk about.

67
00:03:27,580 --> 00:03:30,580
Next, we'll look at. Finally, I'll explain.

68
00:03:30,580 --> 00:03:33,580
That makes the structure very clear.

69
00:03:33,580 --> 00:03:37,580
Exactly. It helps your audience follow your ideas easily.

70
00:03:37,580 --> 00:03:38,580
Let's practice that.

71
00:03:38,580 --> 00:03:39,580
Okay.

72
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:41,580
First, I'll explain the problem.

73
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:43,580
Then, I'll present our solution.

74
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:45,580
Finally, I'll discuss the results.

75
00:03:45,580 --> 00:03:48,580
Perfect. That's a very strong structure.

76
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:51,580
Now, let's talk about language.

77
00:03:51,580 --> 00:03:53,420
What kind of English should we use?

78
00:03:53,420 --> 00:03:57,540
Use simple, professional, and polite language.

79
00:03:57,540 --> 00:03:59,020
Can you give examples?

80
00:03:59,020 --> 00:04:01,940
Instead of saying, this is bad, say,

81
00:04:01,940 --> 00:04:04,380
this is a challenge we need to address.

82
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:06,220
That sounds more professional.

83
00:04:06,220 --> 00:04:09,060
Yes, and instead of we failed,

84
00:04:09,060 --> 00:04:11,740
you can say, we didn't achieve our target.

85
00:04:11,740 --> 00:04:14,580
I see, so we soften the language.

86
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:17,900
Exactly, that's very important in business English.

87
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:20,100
Now, let's talk about confidence.

88
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:22,540
Many people feel nervous when presenting.

89
00:04:22,540 --> 00:04:25,540
Yes, this is one of the biggest challenges.

90
00:04:25,540 --> 00:04:26,740
What can they do?

91
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:28,820
First, practice.

92
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:31,820
The more you practice, the more confident you become.

93
00:04:31,820 --> 00:04:33,020
That makes sense.

94
00:04:33,020 --> 00:04:35,460
Second, prepare your key points.

95
00:04:35,460 --> 00:04:37,300
Don't memorize everything.

96
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:38,820
Why not memorize?

97
00:04:38,820 --> 00:04:42,180
Because if you forget one word, you might panic.

98
00:04:42,180 --> 00:04:43,300
That's true.

99
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:46,940
Instead, understand your ideas and speak naturally.

100
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:47,980
Good advice.

101
00:04:47,980 --> 00:04:49,780
Now, let's do a role play.

102
00:04:49,780 --> 00:04:52,060
you're giving a short presentation.

103
00:04:52,060 --> 00:04:53,460
Okay, I'm ready.

104
00:04:53,460 --> 00:04:54,300
Go ahead.

105
00:04:54,300 --> 00:04:55,540
Good morning everyone.

106
00:04:55,540 --> 00:04:57,340
Thank you for joining today.

107
00:04:57,340 --> 00:04:59,940
I'd like to present our project update.

108
00:04:59,940 --> 00:05:02,380
First, I'll talk about our progress.

109
00:05:02,380 --> 00:05:05,060
Then I'll discuss the challenges we faced.

110
00:05:05,060 --> 00:05:07,620
Finally, I'll share our next steps.

111
00:05:07,620 --> 00:05:10,660
Excellent start, very clear and professional.

112
00:05:10,660 --> 00:05:11,500
Thank you.

113
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:13,060
Now, continue.

114
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:16,580
So far, we have completed 70% of the project.

115
00:05:16,580 --> 00:05:20,500
However, we experienced some delays due to technical issues.

116
00:05:20,500 --> 00:05:24,340
Good. You used HOWEVER to show contrast.

117
00:05:24,340 --> 00:05:25,180
Yes.

118
00:05:25,180 --> 00:05:29,300
That's very important in presentations, or it's like HOWEVER,

119
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:33,020
therefore, and as a result, help connect ideas.

120
00:05:33,020 --> 00:05:33,900
Got it.

121
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:36,580
Now let's talk about body language.

122
00:05:36,580 --> 00:05:39,380
Oh, yes. This is very important.

123
00:05:39,380 --> 00:05:43,620
Even if your English is good, poor body language can affect your presentation.

124
00:05:43,620 --> 00:05:44,780
What should we do?

125
00:05:44,780 --> 00:05:49,540
Maintain eye contact, stand straight, and use natural gestures.

126
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:51,620
And avoid looking at the floor.

127
00:05:51,620 --> 00:05:53,220
Exactly.

128
00:05:53,220 --> 00:05:56,260
Also, don't read everything from your slides.

129
00:05:56,260 --> 00:05:57,980
That's a common mistake.

130
00:05:57,980 --> 00:06:01,940
Yes, your slides should support you, not replace you.

131
00:06:01,940 --> 00:06:03,620
Let's talk about slides.

132
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:05,260
What makes a good slide?

133
00:06:05,260 --> 00:06:09,060
Simple design, short text, and clear visuals.

134
00:06:09,060 --> 00:06:11,340
So not too much information.

135
00:06:11,340 --> 00:06:12,380
Exactly.

136
00:06:12,380 --> 00:06:16,380
If your slide is full of text, people will read instead of listening to you.

137
00:06:16,380 --> 00:06:17,380
That's true.

138
00:06:17,380 --> 00:06:20,380
Now let's talk about handling questions.

139
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:22,380
This is another difficult part.

140
00:06:22,380 --> 00:06:25,380
Yes, but there are simple phrases you can use.

141
00:06:25,380 --> 00:06:26,380
Like what?

142
00:06:26,380 --> 00:06:30,380
If you understand the question, say, that's a great question.

143
00:06:30,380 --> 00:06:31,380
Nice.

144
00:06:31,380 --> 00:06:35,380
If you need time, say, let me think about that for a moment.

145
00:06:35,380 --> 00:06:37,380
Very useful.

146
00:06:37,380 --> 00:06:41,380
And if you don't know the answer, say, I'll need to check that and get back to you.

147
00:06:41,380 --> 00:06:47,940
to you. That sounds professional and honest. Exactly. Never guess or give incorrect information.

148
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:54,740
Now let's do another role play. Okay. I'll ask a question after your presentation. Ready.

149
00:06:54,740 --> 00:07:01,140
Go ahead. In conclusion, our project is progressing well and we expect to complete it by next month.

150
00:07:01,140 --> 00:07:07,140
Thank you for your attention. Great. Now my question. What is the biggest risk for this

151
00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:12,520
project. That's a great question. The biggest risk is potential delays in supply.

152
00:07:12,520 --> 00:07:17,980
However, we are working on alternative solutions. Excellent answer. Thank you.

153
00:07:17,980 --> 00:07:24,220
Now let's talk about common mistakes. Yes, very important. Mistake number one,

154
00:07:24,220 --> 00:07:32,220
speaking too fast. Yes, nervous people do that. Speak slowly and clearly. Mistake

155
00:07:32,220 --> 00:07:35,580
Mistake number two, using difficult vocabulary.

156
00:07:35,580 --> 00:07:37,480
Keep it simple.

157
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:40,740
Mistake number three, reading from slides.

158
00:07:40,740 --> 00:07:43,220
Always engage with your audience.

159
00:07:43,220 --> 00:07:46,140
Mistake number four, no structure.

160
00:07:46,140 --> 00:07:48,580
Always organize your ideas.

161
00:07:48,580 --> 00:07:51,700
Mistake number five, lack of practice.

162
00:07:51,700 --> 00:07:53,820
Practice is the key to improvement.

163
00:07:53,820 --> 00:07:56,380
Now let's talk about real life situations.

164
00:07:56,380 --> 00:07:59,660
Yes, let's imagine you're presenting to your boss.

165
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:00,660
Okay.

166
00:08:00,660 --> 00:08:01,660
What would you say?

167
00:08:01,660 --> 00:08:05,300
Good morning. I'd like to update you on the sales performance.

168
00:08:05,300 --> 00:08:08,340
This month we achieved a 10% increase.

169
00:08:08,340 --> 00:08:11,700
Very good. Now a team presentation.

170
00:08:11,700 --> 00:08:13,600
Go ahead. Hi everyone.

171
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:16,140
Today we'll discuss our upcoming project.

172
00:08:16,140 --> 00:08:18,300
First, we'll review our goals.

173
00:08:18,300 --> 00:08:21,940
Perfect. Now a client presentation.

174
00:08:21,940 --> 00:08:23,380
This is more formal.

175
00:08:23,380 --> 00:08:24,580
Good afternoon.

176
00:08:24,580 --> 00:08:27,160
Thank you for the opportunity to present today.

177
00:08:27,160 --> 00:08:29,780
We are excited to share our proposal with you.

178
00:08:29,780 --> 00:08:32,660
Excellent, very professional tone.

179
00:08:32,660 --> 00:08:33,480
Thank you.

180
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:34,860
Now let's wrap up.

181
00:08:34,860 --> 00:08:36,500
We covered a lot today.

182
00:08:36,500 --> 00:08:38,460
Yes, let's summarize.

183
00:08:38,460 --> 00:08:40,540
Start with a strong opening.

184
00:08:40,540 --> 00:08:42,280
Use clear structure.

185
00:08:42,280 --> 00:08:43,980
Speak simple English.

186
00:08:43,980 --> 00:08:45,500
Practice regularly.

187
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:47,460
Use good body language.

188
00:08:47,460 --> 00:08:50,100
And handle questions confidently.

189
00:08:50,100 --> 00:08:52,980
Most importantly, believe in yourself.

190
00:08:52,980 --> 00:08:56,020
Yes, confidence grows with practice.

191
00:08:56,020 --> 00:08:57,500
Now it's your turn.

192
00:08:57,500 --> 00:09:00,300
