258
00:12:03,980 --> 00:12:08,820
My name is Emma and today I'd like to present our new marketing strategy.

259
00:12:08,820 --> 00:12:09,820
Perfect.

260
00:12:09,820 --> 00:12:11,820
Simple, clear, and professional.

261
00:12:11,820 --> 00:12:15,260
Yes, and you don't need complicated vocabulary.

262
00:12:15,260 --> 00:12:18,540
Now let's talk about structuring your presentation.

263
00:12:18,540 --> 00:12:20,340
This is very important.

264
00:12:20,340 --> 00:12:23,180
A good presentation has a clear structure.

265
00:12:23,180 --> 00:12:25,660
So what does that structure look like?

266
00:12:25,660 --> 00:12:27,500
It has three main parts.

267
00:12:27,500 --> 00:12:30,900
Introduction, main content, and conclusion.

268
00:12:30,900 --> 00:12:32,700
Just like writing an essay.

269
00:12:32,700 --> 00:12:33,820
Exactly.

270
00:12:33,820 --> 00:12:37,460
In the introduction, you tell the audience what you will talk about.

271
00:12:37,460 --> 00:12:40,340
In the main part, you explain your ideas.

272
00:12:40,340 --> 00:12:43,940
And in the conclusion, you summarize your key points.

273
00:12:43,940 --> 00:12:45,940
Let's give a practical example.

274
00:12:45,940 --> 00:12:49,540
Okay, imagine you're presenting sales results.

275
00:12:49,540 --> 00:12:51,460
In the introduction, you say,

276
00:12:51,460 --> 00:12:54,980
today I'll present our sales performance for this quarter.

277
00:12:54,980 --> 00:12:59,220
In the main part, you explain the numbers, trends, and reasons.

278
00:12:59,220 --> 00:13:01,140
And in the conclusion, you say,

279
00:13:01,140 --> 00:13:04,900
in summary, our sales increased by 15%

280
00:13:04,900 --> 00:13:08,020
and we expect continued growth next quarter.

281
00:13:08,020 --> 00:13:09,860
That's a strong structure.

282
00:13:09,860 --> 00:13:12,820
Now let's talk about using simple language.

283
00:13:12,820 --> 00:13:15,780
Yes, this is very important for learners.

284
00:13:15,780 --> 00:13:19,020
Many people think they need very advanced English.

285
00:13:19,020 --> 00:13:22,260
But actually, simple English is more effective.

286
00:13:22,260 --> 00:13:27,580
Exactly. Instead of long sentences, use short in clear ones.

287
00:13:27,580 --> 00:13:29,580
For example, instead of saying,

288
00:13:29,580 --> 00:13:32,700
we have observed a significant upward trend,

289
00:13:32,700 --> 00:13:35,540
you can simply say, our sales are increasing.

290
00:13:35,540 --> 00:13:37,380
Much easier to understand.

291
00:13:37,380 --> 00:13:39,500
Always think about your audience.

292
00:13:39,500 --> 00:13:43,980
Yes, your goal is communication, not showing off vocabulary.

293
00:13:43,980 --> 00:13:47,020
Now let's talk about signposting language.

294
00:13:47,020 --> 00:13:48,780
Ah, very useful.

295
00:13:48,780 --> 00:13:52,780
Signposting means guiding your audience through your presentation.

296
00:13:52,780 --> 00:13:55,740
So they know where they are and what's coming next.

297
00:13:55,740 --> 00:14:00,060
Exactly. For example, first, I'll talk about our goals.

298
00:14:00,060 --> 00:14:02,500
Next, I'll explain our strategy.

299
00:14:02,500 --> 00:14:04,860
Finally, I'll share the results.

300
00:14:04,860 --> 00:14:08,060
These phrases make your presentation easy to follow.

301
00:14:08,060 --> 00:14:10,060
Let's do a mini example.

302
00:14:10,060 --> 00:14:11,060
Go ahead.

303
00:14:11,060 --> 00:14:14,060
First, let's look at last year's performance.

304
00:14:14,060 --> 00:14:17,060
Next, we'll discuss the challenges we faced.

305
00:14:17,060 --> 00:14:20,060
Finally, we'll explore our future plans.

306
00:14:20,060 --> 00:14:22,060
That sounds very professional.

307
00:14:22,060 --> 00:14:24,060
Now let's talk about confidence.

308
00:14:24,060 --> 00:14:27,060
This is a big challenge for many people.

309
00:14:27,060 --> 00:14:30,060
Yes, even native speakers feel nervous sometimes.

310
00:14:30,060 --> 00:14:32,060
So what can we do?

311
00:14:32,060 --> 00:14:34,060
Practice is the key.

312
00:14:34,060 --> 00:14:36,060
Absolutely. The more you practice,

313
00:14:36,060 --> 00:14:42,380
confident you become. Also speak slowly and clearly. That's very important. When

314
00:14:42,380 --> 00:14:47,500
people are nervous they speak too fast. Yes, and then the audience can't understand.

315
00:14:47,500 --> 00:14:54,420
So slow down, pause, and breathe. Now let's talk about body language. Oh yes,

316
00:14:54,420 --> 00:14:59,580
this is very important in presentations. Your body language communicates a lot.

317
00:14:59,580 --> 00:15:02,580
Make eye contact with your audience.

318
00:15:02,580 --> 00:15:05,580
Stand straight and use natural gestures.

319
00:15:05,580 --> 00:15:08,580
Avoid looking at the floor or reading from your slides.

320
00:15:08,580 --> 00:15:10,580
That makes you look less confident.

321
00:15:10,580 --> 00:15:13,580
Now let's talk about using slides.

322
00:15:13,580 --> 00:15:16,580
Many people rely on slides too much.

323
00:15:16,580 --> 00:15:20,580
Yes, slides should support your presentation, not replace you.

324
00:15:20,580 --> 00:15:22,580
Don't write too much text.

325
00:15:22,580 --> 00:15:26,580
Use keywords, images, and simple points.

326
00:15:26,580 --> 00:15:28,580
And don't read directly from the slide.

327
00:15:28,580 --> 00:15:29,580
the slide.

328
00:15:29,580 --> 00:15:30,580
Exactly.

329
00:15:30,580 --> 00:15:33,180
Explain the ideas in your own words.

330
00:15:33,180 --> 00:15:35,180
Now let's do a role play.

331
00:15:35,180 --> 00:15:36,180
Great idea.

